1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,663
Well, l've been talking to managers in a number of businesses,

2
00:00:03,663 --> 00:00:06,500
and reading surveys about the future of management.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:11,236
And what l'm going to present in this seminar is a few ideas about

4
00:00:11,236 --> 00:00:15,330
how the activity is likely to change in the next ten years.

5
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:21,610
It isn't a scientific, statistical analysis - just some ideas for us to discuss.

6
00:00:21,610 --> 00:00:25,819
One area I want to mention is business markets,

7
00:00:25,819 --> 00:00:31,410
and I'm sure a really significant development will be a major increase in competition,

8
00:00:31,410 --> 00:00:36,000
with companies from all round the world trying to sell similar products.

9
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,239
Consumers will have much more choice - for instance, food products sold in Australia

10
00:00:42,239 --> 00:00:48,800
might be manufactured in the USA, China, Finland and dozens of other countries.

11
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:55,724
At the same time, mergers and takeovers mean that governments are actually losing power

12
00:00:55,724 --> 00:00:57,450
to major global corporations.

13
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:02,991
We can probably all think of companies that exert a great deal of influence,

14
00:01:02,991 --> 00:01:05,340
which may be good for consumers.

15
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:10,332
A third point I want to make about markets is that in the rapidly

16
00:01:10,332 --> 00:01:18,110
expanding economies, such as India, China, Brazil and Russia, demand is growing very fast.

17
00:01:18,110 --> 00:01:22,420
This is putting pressure on resources all over the world.

18
00:01:22,420 --> 00:01:27,930
I think businesses are becoming more open to external influences.

19
00:01:27,930 --> 00:01:34,680
In particular, companies are consulting customers more and more before making their business decisions.

20
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:40,044
Companies are finding out what they want and providing it, instead of making products

21
00:01:40,044 --> 00:01:41,370
and then trying to sell them,

22
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:43,600
which is the model of years ago.

23
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:50,840
Another influence is that concerns about the environment will force manufacturers to extend product

24
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:54,470
lifecycles, to reduce the amount of pollution and waste.

25
00:01:54,470 --> 00:01:56,848
And in some cases.

26
00:01:56,848 --> 00:01:59,850
regulation will need to be strengthened.

27
00:01:59,850 --> 00:02:03,472
Many societies are much more fluid and democratic,

28
00:02:03,472 --> 00:02:08,000
and the structure of companies is changing to reflect that.

29
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,565
I think we're going to see a greater emphasis within companies on teams created

30
00:02:13,565 --> 00:02:15,950
with a specific project in mind.

31
00:02:15,950 --> 00:02:21,300
And when they're completed, the teams will be disbanded and new ones formed.

32
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:26,141
More and more people see work as simply one part of their lifestyle,

33
00:02:26,141 --> 00:02:28,375
and not the most important one,

34
00:02:28,375 --> 00:02:33,589
and as the workforce is shrinking in some countries, businesses are having to compete

35
00:02:33,589 --> 00:02:38,430
for staff instead of being able to choose among a lot of applicants.

36
00:02:38,430 --> 00:02:45,035
Typical examples that will attract and retain staff are traditional ones like flexible hours

37
00:02:45,035 --> 00:02:50,697
and - something that has been made possible by advances in technology - remote

38
00:02:50,697 --> 00:02:54,471
working, with people based at their home, abroad,

39
00:02:54,471 --> 00:02:56,830
or almost anywhere they choose.

40
00:02:56,830 --> 00:03:05,370
Management styles will almost certainly continue to change.

41
00:03:05,370 --> 00:03:10,720
Senior managers will require a lot more than the efficiency that they've always needed.

42
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:14,520
Above all they'll need great skills in leadership,

43
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:21,170
so that their organisation can initiate and respond to change in a fast-moving world,

44
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:25,920
where they face lots of competing requirements and potential conflicts.

45
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:32,648
In most of the world, the senior managers of large businesses are mainly men in their fifties

46
00:03:32,648 --> 00:03:33,440
and sixties.

47
00:03:33,440 --> 00:03:38,894
The predominant style of management will almost certainly become more consultative

48
00:03:38,894 --> 00:03:45,340
and collaborative, caused above all, by more women moving into senior management positions.

49
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:50,630
Many of the changes are influenced by developments in the wider economy.

50
00:03:50,630 --> 00:03:53,876
The traditional emphasis of business was manufacturing,

51
00:03:53,876 --> 00:03:58,050
and of course the service sector is very important.

52
00:03:58,050 --> 00:04:03,586
But we shouldn't overlook the growing financial contribution of IP,

53
00:04:03,586 --> 00:04:05,800
that is, intellectual property.

54
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:13,220
Some books and films generate enormous sums from the sale of related DVDs, music,

55
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:14,280
games, clothes,

56
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:15,870
and so on.

57
00:04:15,870 --> 00:04:20,959
Another point I'd like to make is that although l've been talking about companies,

58
00:04:20,959 --> 00:04:26,049
one trend that they have to face is the move away from people working

59
00:04:26,049 --> 00:04:28,230
for the same employer for years.

60
00:04:28,230 --> 00:04:33,428
Instead, more and more people are becoming self-employed, to gain the freedom

61
00:04:33,428 --> 00:04:39,060
and control over their lives that they're unlikely to get from being employed.

62
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:42,185
OK, well that's all I want to say,

63
00:04:42,185 --> 00:04:44,920
so let's open it up for discussion.
